## infra/layer-b/auditd-layerb.rules
## Host-level file-access auditing on the Layer B store path.
## Section 90.3: the compensating control the host-admin holder "cannot silently
## defeat". It exists because "The Layer B access log ... is application-level
## and does not see a root-level file read".
-w /srv/layerb/encrypted -p rwa -k layerb_store
-w /srv/layerb/secrets    -p rwa -k layerb_secrets
## Changing or unloading these rules is itself audited.
-w /etc/audit/rules.d/ -p wa -k layerb_audit_config
## -e 2 makes the running audit configuration immutable until the next reboot
## (auditctl cannot reload or clear rules, and augenrules --load will refuse).
## This is deliberate - it is what makes "changing or unloading these rules is
## itself audited" true rather than aspirational - but it also means the STOP
## rule in L5-03-07 ("re-run L5-P3-07") cannot succeed against a host that has
## already booted with this file loaded: the remedy for a botched rule set is
## a reboot, not a re-run. ship-audit.sh's own STOP rule below states this.
-e 2
